this is a production of cornell  university  i'm joanne labay i'm the vegetable crops  curator  at pgru in geneva new york where the  plant genetic resources unit  and my name is zach stancil and i am the  industrial hemp germplasm curator  although i have a great passion for  vegetable germplasm  um so we're really excited to show you  some of our field increases  this year  we're regenerating a number of annual  and biennial crops and we're really  excited to show you around so  although the primary emphasis of this  work is seed regeneration  to replenish our seed lots for  distribution this work also entails  characterization  and just general  germplasm and genetic diversity  conservation  so welcome  all right so as zach mentioned we have  biennial and annual crops onions are  biennial in the first year they produce  bulbs  and then we have to dig up the bulbs put  them in the barn over the winter and  plant them  the next year  to produce seed so first i'll show you  the first year onions they're here  we have 20 different  types or accessions we call them of  onion that we're regenerating this year  and the plots are laid out on these  plastic strips  and we try to keep a population size of  50 to 100 plants per population so that  we can get the random mating and keep  the diversity so i just wanted to show  you our bees  we rent bees every year  and they're back here we're not going to  get too close because they're active  because it's a warm out  but when we do the pollinations  each accession or each population is  isolated in a nylon mesh cage  and these bees somebody comes in early  in the morning while still cool out and  they transfer these boxes of bees  into the cage  and leave them in there for a few weeks  and all they really need is water and  they feed off the nectar of the flowers  and pollinate  i wanted to show you our second year  ions as i said  these are biennial  so what was planted this is 2020 allium  is onion and plot number 108 and this is  an accession that originated in portugal  what was planted are the bulbs and you  can see the bulbs are kind of  sticking out the soil here  and these will grow the plant and they  will flower  and before they flower  a cage will be put around them so that  they can have the bees put in and be  pollinated but we'll let them grow up a  little bit  before we put the cage over it  so here we have a cabbage increase cage  so this species is brassica ulracia  the dog of the plant world i should  point out it's a species that contains  broccoli cabbage cauliflower kale  brussels sprouts  uh chinese kale etc  these cabbages are kind of unique in  that they have a biennial life cycle so  these seeds were actually started last  year um  2020  grown out one year vegetatively  dug up and stored over winter  basically in a protected but outdoor  environment in upstate new york so they  got quite a bit of chilling hours during  that time so we moved them back out to  the field  and  transplanted them and they're just now  starting to to flower  so  um  many of the materials that we have out  here are not great cabbages they're  they're not uh something you'd be  thrilled to pick up at the grocery store  however that's kind of the it's that's a  feature not a bug um  these represent very genetically and  geographically diverse materials  that you know while they maybe aren't  the highest in horticultural value they  represent some sort of local adaptation  or potential disease or  abiotic stress resistance  so from a plant breeding perspective  it's incredibly valuable to maintain  this these diverse brassica resources  regarding the uniformity you'll notice  that unlike a inbred line or an f1  hybrid population  the plants that we keep in these cages  are  not very uniform and that's actually  uh uh  the the kind of the main point is to  preserve this population level genetic  diversity  um this lack of uniformity is actually  kind of a sign that there is some sort  of segregation occurring  in these populations and so we try to  keep a large enough sample size 50 to  100 plants  minimum to maintain that  especially since  oleraceae is an outcrossing species  having a larger stamp  population size is really essential to  maintain that diversity long term these  plots are our tomato regenerations  tomato is our most popular crop that we  can serve and we have about six thousand  different accessions of tomato  it's our most economically valuable crop  also  cultivated tomato is self fertilizing so  this is the only crop that we regenerate  that we don't use honeybees we don't use  cages  so they're planted in these rows and we  don't need as large population sizes  because they are self-fertilizing we  just need enough plants to get a lot of  seeds so  we do 24 plants per accession for tomato  and we're doing 50 accessions this year  and you see the wooden poles that are in  the plants will be trellised  when they get big so that  the plants are upright and that helps  protect them from things like disease  and it keeps the plots from growing into  each other so we can  make sure we have the identity  of the fruit when we harvest it  celery is another crop that we  conserve  it's also biennial so it gets treated  like the onions this is our first year  celery here  you can see the small young plants and  over here  is a second year salary so  they've been in storage all winter and  they were planted  they have a kind of a big  root on them  so we brought you to one of our seed  processing areas so we this is some of  the equipment we use to  thrash and clean our seeds before  they're packaged for long-term storage  in a  minus 18  freezer one  critical element of maintaining all of  these collections  there's a total of approximately 12 000  unique sessions spanning several hundred  taxa  so it's really vital that we regenerate  those materials periodically  um  if you know we do  a hundred  sessions a year then that's  120 years to  flip over the entire population or the  entire collection  so it's really important that we develop  new collaborations going forth  please feel free to contact joanna or i  if you have the capacity or willingness  to help us with those we'd love to have  those conversations with you  so we have  three primary  core groups of stakeholders that utilize  national plant germ plasm system  collection so  a primary user is plant breeders who  like i mentioned before  you know will find a sessions that are  locally adapted or have unique origins  or are valuable  in some regard for their breeding  programs  people conducting very basic plant  science research will will often request  our entire collection to screen it to  for example uh  conduct association mapping for a  certain gene  and then there's a lot of educational  value in these collections as well so  certain higher institutions will  use these materials i actually pulled  some of the brassica collection to show  a domestication timeline presentation at  cornell which is a lot of fun  so your biggest limitation is your  imagination when you have this much  genetic diversity  giving you a full tour would probably  take the the whole uh  the whole video session today we didn't  include uh the asparagus collection  which i'm working on regenerating um  we also keep many leafy brassicas  the radish collection is kept here  we have the buckwheat collection as well  and i'm  and yeah thank you julian the winter  squash collection is kept here as well  if you are a plant breeder or someone  doing research or  higher education and you're interested  in obtaining seed or germplasm from the  national plant germplasm system we have  a nice database a web portal online  called grim global  you go there create an account and you  can order  seed  we ship seed all around the world  and if there is any  information about the accession the  passport data  that will be in that database and you  can find it on green global if there's  any images same thing you'll see it  there  this has been a production of cornell  university on the web at cornell.edu

